hole number part by the denominator and add the numerator:\n$33\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{(33 \\times 3) + 1}{3} = \\frac{99 + 1}{3} = \\frac{100}{3}$\n\nGear 2 turns 45 times in a minute.\n\nNow, we need to find the LCM of $\\frac{100}{3}$ and 45. To do this, we first find the LCM of the numerators (100 and 45) and the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the denominators (3 and 1). Since 1 is a factor of every number, the GCD of 3 and 1 is 1.\n\nThe LCM of 100 and 45 is the product of the two numbers divided by their GCD. However, since the GCD is 1, we can simply multiply the two numbers:\nLCM(100, 45) = (100 * 45) / 1 = 4500\n\nNow, we need to find the LCM of 3 and 1. As mentioned earlier, the GCD of 3 and 1 is 1. Therefore, the LCM is simply the product of the two numbers:\nLCM(3, 1) = (3 * 1) / 1 = 3\n\nNow, we multiply the LCM of the numerators and the LCM of the denominators to get the LCM of $\\frac{100}{3}$ and 45:\nLCM($\\frac{100}{3}$, 45) = (LCM(100, 45)) / LCM(3, 1) = 4500 / 3 = 1500\n\nThis means that both gears will have their marks pointing due north again after 1500 minutes.\n\nTo convert this to seconds, we multiply by 60 (since there are 60 seconds in a minute):\n1500 minutes * 60 seconds/minute = 90000 seconds\n\nTherefore, the two gears will next have both their marks pointing due north after $\\boxed{90000}$ seconds.", "decode_seconds": 22.99202098697424, "draft_calls": 0, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 112, "method": "native_ar", "output_hash": "bd3b0483eec558f9acc15afdc07b421c6467e4db498a47f49c1255df09689253", "output_token_ids": [1271, 1505, 279, 892, 994, 2225, 54260, 617, 872, 15785, 22364, 4245, 10411, 1578, 11, 584, 1205, 311, 1505, 279, 3325, 4279, 5361, 320, 8724, 44, 8, 315, 279, 1396, 315, 3115, 1855, 14787, 10800, 304, 264, 9568, 382, 83884, 220, 16, 10800, 400, 1644, 59, 38118, 90, 16, 15523, 18, 32816, 3115, 304, 264, 9568, 13, 2057, 5625, 420, 311, 459, 41086, 19983, 11, 584, 31370, 279, 4459, 1396, 961, 555, 279, 48012, 323, 